{"version":2,"kind":"Article","sha256":"fb51f881ec9686151d37f75715bb4f575ba970845dfa242b9cab2f0ffd1f3451","slug":"why-study","location":"/01-why-study.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Lecture 1.1: Why Study the History of Christianity?","authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Eric Araujo","given":"Eric","family":"Araujo"},"name":"Eric Araujo","id":"contributors-01-why-study-generated-uid-0"}],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"01-why-study.md","url":"/01-why-study-7370474026ad16c1f72ad57e9bf4f0ad.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. How to Study Church History","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"UvFr0mdEn7"}],"identifier":"id-3-how-to-study-church-history","label":"3. How to Study Church History","html_id":"id-3-how-to-study-church-history","implicit":true,"key":"wcOXYzsQZM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A few quotes were presented to guide us in our study of church history:","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"fvFHoTc7vJ"}],"key":"u7kavRh61Y"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In modernity, church history has often been taught not “in terms of the missionary advance in successive encounters of the gospel with different forms of human culture and society, but rather as a story of the doctrinal and other conflicts within the life of the church.” (L. Newbigin)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"NyS52KGRrR"}],"key":"NGLwdDgYFq"}],"key":"m9Z8jamg9G"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I wish someone had told me in seminary that the Christological and Trinitarian controversies of the second and third century were first and foremost issues of contextualization. (L. Newbigin)","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"OehxrYce2K"}],"key":"E0eQ8y56JI"}],"key":"FnJQsaNs2d"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We have to ask in all sincerity whether the study of the history of the church ought not to be completely redesigned. (David Bosch)","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"rh9rPmXvJ9"}],"key":"HJg3vnCmnu"}],"key":"YWYwJyAXda"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"These quotes highlight the importance of approaching church history with a missional and contextual perspective. We should not just focus on doctrinal disputes but also on how the gospel has been communicated and lived out in different cultural settings.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"y7UHBOsrk1"}],"key":"kGZSektqXQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To provide a tool for studying church history, Prof. Rylaarsdam introduced us the two principles for missional encounters with culture:","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"UuWyllZ788"}],"key":"t761ZOs544"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The indigenizing principle:","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"n9vlEcNa0N"}],"key":"hZjKJWEmPW"},{"type":"text","value":" Christianity enters a culture and finds customs that embody the way of Christ. Christians celebrate the culture. They are residents in it.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"W1ypvrngm4"}],"key":"SRYUS9mFpD"}],"key":"BCZWDB1QgH"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The pilgrim principle:","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"CUwAKH0Wtb"}],"key":"Oy8LzSwNvi"},{"type":"text","value":" Christianity enters a culture and finds ways in which the culture contradicts the way and teachings of Christ. Christians seek to embody alternatives. They are not fully at home; they are ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"gV9iMYRmuS"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"resident aliens","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"Ea2TLt3CZu"}],"key":"kDz5O9LEGd"},{"type":"text","value":" (","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"pfdAu0QhlQ"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"